Byron A Jeff wrote:

> We are certainly on the same page.

Well, close. The difference is where the emphasis is
put. I put buffing up my pet(s) _first_, which
perhaps I didn't explain well enough.

I'll delay money gathering expeditions or searching
for armor _until_ my pet has say 44 HP or more, when
I can measure that, or is a "large" cat/dog or
"warhorse". That means we might wander around DLVL 1
or 2 long past the point where the two levels are
entirely explored, to let the pet continue adding HP
by killing wandering and sessile monsters, which is
why I have to put so much attention into snatching
corpses from my pet(s).

A handful of corpses between food prayers is usually
enough of a time extension before going "weak" to
keep the deity happy about being pestered for
nutrition.

This strategy is made a lot easier that I'm playing
pretty strictly healers right now, and for a month
or two, so that I can use my stethescope to check
pet health, and my low level healing spell to keep
the pet's HPs maxed.

However, it works almost as well playing any
character, one just needs to be more thoughtful
about what condition the pet is likely to enjoy at
any point, since you can't measure it directly.

Once the pet is buffed up, further exploration to
fulfill the money and armor goals is a lot safer.

